The profit function is \[
\begin{aligned}
P(x) &= R(x) - C(x) \\
&= 5x - (100 + 2x + 0.1x^2) \\
&= -0.1x^2 + 3x - 100
\end{aligned}
\]. This is a downward-opening parabola. The maximum profit occurs at the vertex, where \[
\begin{aligned}
x &= -\frac{b}{2a} \\
&= -\frac{3}{2(-0.1)} \\
&= 15
\end{aligned}
\]. Thus, the production level that maximizes profit is \(15\) hundred units.

First, calculate the mean of the data set: \[
\begin{aligned}
\mu &= \frac{10 + 12 + 14 + 16 + 18}{5} \\
&= \frac{70}{5} \\
&= 14
\end{aligned}
\]. Next, calculate the squared differences from the mean for each data point: \((10-14)^2 = 16\), \((12-14)^2 = 4\), \((14-14)^2 = 0\), \((16-14)^2 = 4\), \((18-14)^2 = 16\). The variance is the average of these squared differences: \[
\begin{aligned}
\sigma^2 &= \frac{16 + 4 + 0 + 4 + 16}{5} \\
&= \frac{40}{5} \\
&= 8
\end{aligned}
\].
